  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Fog development likely late tonight into Fri\nmorning with mostly SKC, decoupled winds, and RHs already \naround 100%. Fog may become dense at times and a dense fog \nadvisory may be needed in spots. REFS and HREF data suggest A \n50-70% chance for VIS < 1/4mi across most of ENC outside of the\nimmediate coast and far Nern tier of counties. As a result, \nhave a mix of patchy and areas of fog in the grids across much \nof ENC in the early morning hours.\n\nUpper ridging will peak over the Mid- Atlantic Friday before \npushing offshore with the approach of an upper level trough in \nthe Ohio River Valley that will push east as well. High temps \ncould flirt with record highs given the set up (SEE CLIMATE \nSECTION FOR MORE DETAILS). \n\nFor reference, normal highs in early October are around 80 degrees, \nand forecast highs are in the low 90s inland. Persistent \nsoutherly flow and increasing low- level moisture will lead to \nmuggier lows as well, well above Normal, mid to upper 60s FRI \nand SAT mornings.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...No real changes in forecast thinking this weekend \nas precipitation chances return as Gulf moisture opens up and a\ncold front drapes across the Sern Appalachians and pushes \nSEward slowly on SAT. As the next wave aloft approaches, the \nmoisture will squeeze into ENC, elevating rain chances for the \nweekend. While thunderstorms will be possible, the threat for \nsevere weather is very low as ML CAPES are quite low,\nunsurprising for early Oct.\n\nThe front loses steam as it approaches the coast and stalls, \nahead of a second reinforcing front spurred from the Great Lakes\nby a stronger trough aloft. For now, ensemble guidance has\nincreased to 60-75% chance of exceeding 1\" of storm total rain \nwith this system. Any rain will be helpful, especially for the \ncoastal plain of ENC that is currently in moderate to extreme \ndrought.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Precip chances now diminish quickly late Sun\nnight and by Mon morning should be pushing off the OBX with \ndrying conditions expected early next week. Stiff NEerly breeze \n(peaking MON night into TUE) will lead to building seas and \nelevated water levels. Astronomical tide levels will luckily be \non the way down, but seas building to 7ft, though relatively\nchoppy periodicity of 7 sec, but enough to lead to further \nbeach and dune erosion concerns across the areas with severely \ndiminished dune structures. Latest 01/12Z model suite has\ntrended a bit lower with winds fortunately, and will have to\nwatch if this is a continuing trend. strongest winds of 20-25 kt\nwith higher gusts now expected Mon night. Winds remain out of \nthe N-NE into late- week, but speeds diminish to 10-15kt later \nTue and beyond.",
